# Architecture

This template uses **MVVM with a feature-package layout**. Follow it by default.

Escalate to **MVI** only when a feature is state-heavy, or when explicitly requested.

Never mix both inside one feature.

## MVVM Baseline (default)

Use for ordinary screens: lists, forms, settings, simple flows.

```
presentation/<feature>/
    Fragment<Feature>.kt        // renders state, forwards user actions
    <Feature>ViewModel.kt       // holds state, no Android framework leaks
    adapters/                   // ListAdapter + DiffUtil
    models/                     // UI models, data classes
    interfaces/                 // click listeners / callbacks
    repository/                 // data access for this feature only
```

Rules

- Fragment/Activity render UI and forward events. No business logic.
- ViewModel owns state and exposes it as `StateFlow` (or `LiveData` when the screen already uses it).
- Repository owns data access. A ViewModel never touches SharedPreferences, files, network, or DB directly.
- A model shared by several features goes in a shared package, not inside one feature.
- Data used app-wide goes in `common/repository`, `common/viewmodel`, or `storage/`.

## MVI (state-heavy features)

Use when a screen has many interdependent states, complex async flows, or one-shot effects.

```
presentation/<feature>/
    Fragment<Feature>.kt
    <Feature>ViewModel.kt
    state/<Feature>UiState.kt   // immutable data class
    intent/<Feature>Intent.kt   // sealed interface
    effect/<Feature>Effect.kt   // sealed interface
```

Rules

- One immutable `UiState` data class per screen.
- User actions enter the ViewModel as a single `onIntent(intent)` entry point.
- State via `StateFlow`, one-shot effects (navigation, toast, dialog) via `SharedFlow` or `Channel`.
- Never expose `MutableStateFlow` / `MutableSharedFlow` publicly. Expose `asStateFlow()` / `asSharedFlow()`.

## Domain Layer

The template has no domain layer. Do not create one for a simple feature.

Introduce `domain/` (models, repository interfaces, use cases) only when

- business logic is reused by more than one screen, or
- logic is complex enough to deserve isolated unit tests, or
- the user explicitly asks for Clean Architecture.

When introduced, the domain layer must not depend on the Android framework.

## Principles

- Follow SOLID.
- Follow Separation of Concerns.
- Prefer immutable state.
- Keep dependencies one-directional: presentation -> (domain) -> data.
- Reuse existing architecture. Never redesign an existing screen while adding a feature.
- Avoid tightly coupled classes and giant classes.
